<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guru Granth Sahib</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1604">1604</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Adi Granth, Pothi Sahib, Ād Granth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guru Granth Sahib is the central holy scripture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a>, regarded by Sikhs as the final, sovereign and eternal Guru after the ten human gurus. Its first rendition, the Adi Granth, was compiled by the fifth guru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a>, completed in 1604 and installed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-temple">Golden Temple</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a>, the tenth guru, added the hymns of Guru Tegh Bahadur and affirmed the text as his successor in 1708. It has 1,430 angs (pages), arranged by 31 main rāgas, and is written in the Gurmukhi script. Composed mainly by six <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-gurus">Sikh gurus</a>, it also holds the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Bhakti sants such as Kabir and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namdev">Namdev</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint Sheikh Farid.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib.jpg" alt="Guru Granth Sahib" width="330" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_Guru_Granth_Sahib_Nishan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fifth Guru, Arjan, built the Harmandir Sahib, later known as the Golden Temple, in the middle of the pool, and in 1604 compiled the <b>Adi Granth</b>, the first Sikh scripture, gathering the hymns of the Gurus and of Hindu and Muslim saints.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1604 Guru Arjan, the fifth Sikh Guru, placed hymns of Kabir, Namdev, Ravidas and other saints in the <b>Adi Granth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Ravidas, a poet-saint born to a leather-working family, preached against caste, and his verses entered the Sikh scripture, the <b>Adi Granth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Japji Sahib is the composition that opens the <b>Guru Granth Sahib</b>, the scripture of the Sikhs.</blockquote><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a></li><li><blockquote>Guru Arjan, the fifth Sikh guru, completed the compilation of the <b>Adi Granth</b>, the first rendition of the Sikh scripture later known as the Guru Granth Sahib.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-29">29 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
